All 8 hangs are fixed and verified. Here's the full picture.

## Root cause (one bug, one line region)

Using cuda-gdb on the live hung kernel (`info cuda warps`) I saw the deadlock immediately: in block (0,0,0), 15 warps were parked at the final `__syncthreads`, but **warp 3 was stuck with a partial active-lane mask** (`0x0001ffff` — only lanes 0–16). Warp 3 covers rows 96–127, and `seqlen_q=113`, so lanes 0–16 (rows 96–112) are valid and 17–31 (rows 113–127) are not.

That warp diverged at `if(qr<seqlen_q)` in the `fwd_kernel_ws2` epilogue — and **inside that branch it called `tmem_wait_ld()` (`tcgen05.wait::ld.sync.aligned`), a warp-convergent op that hangs forever when only part of the warp enters**. So any prefill shape whose `seqlen_q % 32 != 0` deadlocks its boundary warp, which then wedges the whole CTA at `__syncthreads`.

This exactly explains the hang set: `oddseq` (q=113), `deg_q239k1` (q=239), and every standard varlen config (the ragged mix contains q=113 and q=108). `varlen_hd192_dv128` survived only because hd192 routes to a *different* kernel (`ws1`) that already reads tmem convergently. `synccheck` and `memcheck` were both clean — it's a pure logical deadlock, not an illegal op.

## The fix

In `fwd_kernel_ws2`'s epilogue, run the tmem read + `tcgen05.wait` + scaling on the **full warp** and predicate only the global O/LSE stores — mirroring what the already-correct `ws1` and `ws2_paged` kernels do:

```3568:3593:/home/yak/fa3-cuda/kernels/fa4_blackwell.cu
        // tmem read + tcgen05.wait::ld.sync.aligned MUST run on the FULL warp (convergent): guarding them
        // behind `qr<seqlen_q` deadlocks the boundary warp when seqlen_q%32!=0 (partial-warp divergence on
        // the aligned wait). So read/scale unconditionally; predicate only the global stores (mirrors ws1).
        const bool ow=(qr<seqlen_q);
        ...
        for(int g=0;g<HDV;g+=32){ uint32_t ov[32]; tmem_ld32(...); tmem_wait_ld();
            for(int t=0;t<32;t+=2) pmul2(ov[t],ov[t+1],inv2);
            if(ow){ /* only the uint4/scalar global stores are guarded */ } }
        if(qr<seqlen_q) lse[...] = ...;
```

## Verification

- **All 8 previously-hanging configs now pass correctness vs the FA4 oracle** (perf ratios): `oddseq` 0.72x, `deg_q239k1` 0.74x, `varlen_hd128_causal` 0.70x, `varlen_hd128_noncausal` 0.73x, `varlen_hd64_causal` 0.71x, `varlen_hd128_mqa` 0.69x, `varlen_hd128_local` 0.72x, `varlen_hd128_sink` 0.64x.
- **No regression**: the 15 previously-passing ws2 dense/varlen configs are unchanged within noise (`hd128_causal` 0.624x, `large4k` 0.550x, `noncausal` 0.731x, etc.). Batch result: **23/23 correct, 0 wrong, 0 hangs.**
- **compute-sanitizer memcheck: 0 errors** on the boundary case (the extra tmem reads for invalid rows are in-bounds; guarded stores are skipped).

The change is isolated to `fwd_kernel_ws2`; the paged/decode kernels already used the safe pattern (confirmed — that's why `prefill3577` q=3577 and `qlen120`/`qlen8` never hung). I also updated the distillation notes and removed the temporary debug harness I'd added. The only remaining non-runnable configs are the 10 build-time `UNSUP` ones (fp8 paths and head-dim ≥ 192), which are a separate "not built" matter, not deadlocks.
